.TH V32AS-LANGUAGE 7 "2026-11-05" "v32as 20261105-dev" "Vircon32 assembler manual"
.SH NAME
v32as\-language \- summary of the assembly language and preprocessor accepted by v32as
.SH DESCRIPTION
This page summarises the language that
.BR v32as (1)
reads.
It is a reminder, not a specification: the README, the preprocessor notes
.RI ( docs/PREPROCESSOR.md )
and the object format
.RI ( docs/OBJECT.md )
in the source distribution are the complete references.
The instruction set itself is that of the Vircon32 console; the instructions,
registers and I/O port names are those of the official assembler.
.PP
The language has two modes.
The default (\(lqenhanced\(rq) accepts everything below.
With
.B \-\-standard
every extension is off and only the official assembler's language is accepted;
the differences are listed at the end.
.SH SYNTAX
.SS "Statements and comments"
A statement is an instruction or a data directive, optionally preceded by one or
more labels, and ends at the end of the line.
A label and a statement may share a line.
A comment starts with a semicolon and runs to the end of the line.
Mnemonics, register names, directive names and I/O port names are
case\-insensitive.
.PP
.nf
.RS
_loop:  iadd R0, 1        ; a label, an instruction, a comment
        jt   R0, _loop
.RE
.fi
.SS "Labels"
A label starts with an underscore:
.BR _name: .
Labels may be used before they are defined.
Wherever a label is accepted as a value,
.I label+integer
and
.I label\-integer
are too (not with
.BR \-\-standard ).
After a global label,
.B .loop:
defines a local label and
.B .loop
refers to it (it is the label
.BR _draw__loop
after
.BR _draw: ).
.SS "Registers and operands"
The CPU registers are R0 to R15; CR, SR, DR, BP and SP are other names for R11
to R15.
Immediate values, registers, memory operands in square brackets
.RB ( [R1] ", " [R1+4] ", " [_label] ),
I/O port names (for example
.BR TIM_CurrentDate ,
.BR GPU_Command )
and the port value names (for example
.BR GPUCommand_ClearScreen )
are written as in the official assembler.
.PP
The internal registers IP, IR and IV can be used with
.BR MOV ,
which expands to exactly one real instruction (not with
.BR \-\-standard ):
.PP
.nf
.RS
MOV Rn, IP       address of the next instruction
MOV Rn, IR       the encoding of the instruction being executed
MOV IP, Rn       a jump
MOV IR, Rn       overwrite the next instruction word
MOV IV, Rn       overwrite the next instruction's immediate
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B "MOV Rn, IV"
is rejected.
The writes to IR and IV patch code at run time, so that code must run from RAM
(the assembler warns).
.SS "Numbers"
.TP
.B 42
Decimal.
.TP
.B #42
Decimal with a prefix (also
.BR #\-3 ).
.TP
.BR 0xFF ", " FFh
Hexadecimal (the
.B h
suffix form is an extension).
.TP
.B 0b1010
Binary.
.TP
.B 0777
A leading zero means octal (511), but decimal in the official assembler;
.B v32as
warns about every such literal.
.TP
.B 3.14
A floating point number (32 bits).
.TP
.B \(aqA\(aq
A character literal, an integer; it takes the same escapes as strings.
.TP
.BR true ", " false
1 and 0.
.PP
Strings are in double quotes and take the escapes
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \e\(aq ,
.B \e"
and
.BR \exHH .
.SS "Data directives"
.TP
.B integer, float, pointer
A comma separated list, one word per element.
.B pointer
takes labels (also
.IR label+N ).
.TP
.B string
One word per character plus a terminating 0.
.TP
.B zstring
Four characters per word, the first in the low byte, zero terminated, the last
word zero padded.
Not in the official assembler.
.TP
.B datafile \(dqpath\(dq
Reads the file as little\-endian words.
The file is looked up as an absolute path; in the directory of the file holding
the directive; in each
.B \-I
directory; in the current directory.
With
.B \-\-standard
the
.B \-I
directories are not searched.
.TP
.B global _a, _b
.TP
.B extern _a
Objects (see
.BR v32asld (1)):
the label is defined here and visible to other objects, or defined in another
object.
Plain names without a percent sign; errors under
.BR \-\-standard .
.TP
.B datasym _NAME
Declares a name whose value is 0, so
.I _NAME+17
is a word holding 17 that the relocation file
.RB ( "\-r" )
marks
.B D
(a reference into the module's data).
.SS "Debug comments"
Comment lines of their own that start with
.B ;@
are read by
.B \-g
(not with
.BR \-\-standard ):
.TP
.B ;@src FILE:LINE
.TP
.B ;@src \-
Source position for the instructions that follow.
.TP
.B ;@var NAME loc=... type=...
.TP
.B ;@scope {
.TP
.B ;@scope }
.TP
.B ;@fn __function_NAME
Local variables, their scopes, and the end of a function.
.PP
See
.BR v32as (1)
for the files they produce.
.SH PREPROCESSOR
A directive is a line whose first non\-blank character is
.BR % .
Directive names are case\-insensitive; macro names are not.
Macros are replaced only in code, never inside strings, character literals,
comments or numbers.
.SS "In the official assembler"
.nf
.RS
%define NAME tokens...     %undef NAME
%ifdef NAME  %ifndef NAME  %else  %endif
%include "file"            %error "message"   %warning "message"
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B %define
is expanded where it is used.
.B %include
paths are relative to the including file; the nesting limit is 20.
A skipped
.B %ifdef
block drops every line, including unknown directives.
.SS "Extensions"
.TP
.B "%define F(a, b) ..."
Function\-like macros, with
.B #x
(stringize),
.B a ## b
(join),
.B ...
and
.BR __VA_ARGS__ .
.TP
.BR "%if %elif %elifdef %elifndef"
Conditionals with an expression: 32\-bit integers and floats,
.BR defined(NAME) ,
C operators with C precedence, and the functions
.BR int ,
.BR float ,
.BR abs ,
.BR min ,
.BR max ,
.BR floor ,
.B ceil
and
.BR round .
.TP
.BR "%assign NAME expr" ", " "%eval NAME expr"
Compute a value now.
.TP
.B %eval(expr)
Replaced anywhere in a line by the value of the expression.
.TP
.BR "%idefine %xdefine %ixdefine"
Case\-insensitive define; define expanded now; both.
.TP
.B %include <name>
Searches only the
.B \-I
directories.
.B %include "name"
tries the including file's directory first.
.B %includeonce
and
.B "%pragma once"
make later includes of the file no\-ops.
.TP
.BR "%macro %imacro %endmacro %unmacro"
Statement macros with named parameters, defaults, a rest parameter, and
.BR %0 ,
.BR %1 ,
.BR %* ;
.B %exitmacro
leaves one.
.TP
.BR "%rep %endrep %exitrep %@ %rotate"
Loops (up to 1,000,000 iterations) and the iteration number.
.TP
.B %%name
A label private to one macro expansion.
.TP
.BR "%ifreg %ifsysreg %ifnum %ifstr %iflabel %ifport %ifidn %ifidni %ifempty %ifmacro %ifctx"
Operand tests, each also as
.B %elif...
and negated with
.B n
(for example
.BR %ifnreg ).
.TP
.BR "%strlen %substr %strcat %defstr"
String directives.
.TP
.BR "%push %pop %repl, %$x"
Contexts and names private to a context.
.TP
.B "%pragma shadow"
Allow a macro with the name of an instruction without a warning.
.PP
Predefined symbols are
.BR __FILE__ ,
.BR __LINE__ ,
.BR __COUNTER__ ,
.BR __INCLUDE_LEVEL__ ,
.BR __V32AS__ ,
.BR __BASE__ ,
and one of
.BR __BIOS__ ,
.BR __CART__ ,
.B __ORIGIN__
or
.BR __NONE__ .
.PP
Limits: 64 levels of macro expansion, about 4 million replayed lines per run, 20
levels of
.BR %include .
.SH "ENHANCED SYNTAX AND \-\-STANDARD"
Everything in the following list is an error, or does not exist, under
.BR \-\-standard :
.IP \(bu 2
the number forms
.BR 0b101 ,
.BR FFh ,
.B #42
and
.B 0X1F
(a leading zero is then decimal);
.IP \(bu 2
the names IP, IR, IV and the directive
.BR zstring ;
.IP \(bu 2
all preprocessor directives other than the official ones above, and they must be
in lowercase; function\-like macros,
.B .local
labels and
symbols such as
.BR __FILE__ ;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B %define
whose value contains its own name or a percent sign;
.IP \(bu 2
.B %include
other than
.BR \(dqfile\(dq ,
and any use of
.BR \-I
for it;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR label+integer ,
.BR global ,
.BR extern ,
.B datasym
and the
.B ;@
comments as debug input (they are plain comments);
.IP \(bu 2
more than one input file.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-standard
an unterminated
.B %if
is only a warning.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
